{"sourceSha256": "e6a1fee6bcf965d268ccc86de3672f5e1b3f830b060837c6b0926ebd6bea6723", "text": "‘hip ifay peat ie\n\npen ae at i: SRS CR Sere.\nROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 1 MEDICINE\n\nSETTING Suburban Medical Clinic |\n\nPATIENT You are 41 years old and were recently diagnosed with bipolar disorder (a\npsychiatric condition characterised by extreme mood changes). You have been on\na medication called Zolott (sertraline) for a week now, and are feeling great. In fact,\nyou feel so much better that you don’t think you need to continue taking your\nmedication.\n\n| TASK ¢ Explain to your doctor how much better you fee! since you began taking Zoloft.\n\n* Maintain that the medication has worked very well and you are not experiencing\nany side effects, trouble sleeping or suicidal thoughts.\n\n¢ Suggest that, since you fee! quite normal again, there is no need for you to\ncontinue taking your medication.\n\ne Be resistant to continuing the medication. You do not want people to find out you\nhave a psychiatric disorder as they may think differently of you.\n\n© Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment ; — MAY 2015\n\nSee\n\n- Sn TTT ee a ee et\n\nCANDIDATE CARD NO. 1 MEDICINE\n\nSETTING Suburban Medical Clinic\n\nDOCTOR Your 41-year-old patient has come for a review appointment. The patient was\nrecently diagnosed with bipolar disorder (a psychiatric condition characterised by\nextreme mood changes). He/she has been prescribed sertraline (Zoloft) and his/her\ncondition has stabilised. The patient wishes to discontinue treatment.\n\nTASK e Discuss how the medication is working.\n\ne Ensure the patient has not been experiencing trouble sleeping, suicidal thoughts\nor other potential side effects.\n\n* Explain the importance of continuing to take the medication as prescribed (@.g.,\nstopping medication can cause return of symptoms, etc.).\n\n¢ Try to convince the patient to continue the treatment (e.g., medication appears to\nbe working, etc.).\n\n* Reassure the patient, who is anxious that people will think differently of him/her if\nthey find out about his/her bipolar disorder.\n\n@ Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment eee La", "pages": [{"number": 1, "text": "‘hip ifay peat ie\n\npen ae at i: SRS CR Sere.\nROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 1 MEDICINE\n\nSETTING Suburban Medical Clinic |\n\nPATIENT You are 41 years old and were recently diagnosed with bipolar disorder (a\npsychiatric condition characterised by extreme mood changes). You have been on\na medication called Zolott (sertraline) for a week now, and are feeling great. In fact,\nyou feel so much better that you don’t think you need to continue taking your\nmedication.\n\n| TASK ¢ Explain to your doctor how much better you fee! since you began taking Zoloft.\n\n* Maintain that the medication has worked very well and you are not experiencing\nany side effects, trouble sleeping or suicidal thoughts.\n\n¢ Suggest that, since you fee! quite normal again, there is no need for you to\ncontinue taking your medication.\n\ne Be resistant to continuing the medication. You do not want people to find out you\nhave a psychiatric disorder as they may think differently of you.\n\n© Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment ; — MAY 2015\n\nSee\n\n- Sn TTT ee a ee et\n\nCANDIDATE CARD NO. 1 MEDICINE\n\nSETTING Suburban Medical Clinic\n\nDOCTOR Your 41-year-old patient has come for a review appointment. The patient was\nrecently diagnosed with bipolar disorder (a psychiatric condition characterised by\nextreme mood changes). He/she has been prescribed sertraline (Zoloft) and his/her\ncondition has stabilised. The patient wishes to discontinue treatment.\n\nTASK e Discuss how the medication is working.\n\ne Ensure the patient has not been experiencing trouble sleeping, suicidal thoughts\nor other potential side effects.\n\n* Explain the importance of continuing to take the medication as prescribed (@.g.,\nstopping medication can cause return of symptoms, etc.).\n\n¢ Try to convince the patient to continue the treatment (e.g., medication appears to\nbe working, etc.).\n\n* Reassure the patient, who is anxious that people will think differently of him/her if\nthey find out about his/her bipolar disorder.\n\n@ Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment eee La", "extraction": "ocr", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 2163.0, "height": 2800.0, "blocks": [{"text": "‘hip ifay peat ie", "lines": [{"text": "‘hip ifay peat ie", "x": 0.67175, "y": 0.01571, "width": 0.07536, "height": 0.01107, "confidence": 0.168, "words": [{"text": "‘hip", "x": 0.67175, "width": 0.00786, "confidence": 0.319}, {"text": "ifay", "x": 0.68377, "width": 0.01017, "confidence": 0.056}, {"text": "peat", "x": 0.69718, "width": 0.01248, "confidence": 0.0}, {"text": "ie", "x": 0.73, "width": 0.01711, "confidence": 0.297}], "uncertainWords": ["‘hip", "ifay", "peat", "ie"]}]}, {"text": "pen ae at i: SRS CR Sere.\nROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 1 MEDICINE", "lines": [{"text": "pen ae at i: SRS CR Sere.", "x": 0.05594, "y": 0.01536, "width": 0.92973, "height": 0.03, "confidence": 0.345, "words": [{"text": "pen", "x": 0.05594, "width": 0.043, "confidence": 0.425}, {"text": "ae", "x": 0.16089, "width": 0.02589, "confidence": 0.504}, {"text": "at", "x": 0.21313, "width": 0.04068, "confidence": 0.431}, {"text": "i:", "x": 0.33564, "width": 0.02543, "confidence": 0.471}, {"text": "SRS", "x": 0.39945, "width": 0.18169, "confidence": 0.077}, {"text": "CR", "x": 0.62321, "width": 0.04392, "confidence": 0.24}, {"text": "Sere.", "x": 0.7656, "width": 0.22006, "confidence": 0.27}], "uncertainWords": ["pen", "ae", "at", "i:", "SRS", "CR", "Sere."]}, {"text": "ROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 1 MEDICINE", "x": 0.03421, "y": 0.05, "width": 0.70596, "height": 0.01321, "confidence": 0.954, "words": [{"text": "ROLEPLAYER", "x": 0.03421, "width": 0.15395, "confidence": 0.929}, {"text": "CARD", "x": 0.19602, "width": 0.06519, "confidence": 0.966}, {"text": "NO.", "x": 0.27231, "width": 0.03745, "confidence": 0.962}, {"text": "1", "x": 0.31946, "width": 0.00786, "confidence